WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.930 --> 00:00:02.550
Film Courage: Do you have a writing routine
that you follow?

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:07.959
David Wappel, Screenwriter: I have two answers
for if I have a writing routine that I follow

00:00:07.959 --> 00:00:12.400
and one is yes I have a writing routine.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:17.610
Two is I don’t always follow that writing
routine.

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:25.660
The best work I’ve ever done is when I followed
that routine but for some reason I don’t

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:27.390
follow it all of the time.

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:31.940
It’s tough and sometimes it is because of
whatever else I’ve got going on in my life,

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:38.329
that routine that I liked and really worked
for me didn’t, it’s no longer sort of

00:00:38.329 --> 00:00:41.010
the option for what I have going on right
now.

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:47.350
Other times it’s just tough to sit down
at a desk and write even though I know I already

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:55.629
have a plan to do that, it’s just tough
when you don’t want to and so sometimes

00:00:55.629 --> 00:00:56.629
I don’t.

00:00:56.629 --> 00:00:58.610
I fail at sitting down and writing.

00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:03.500
But when I was following it it was great.

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:08.470
When I do follow it it’s great because it
forces me to write and the routine was basically

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:16.040
I would get up, two hours in the morning I
would write and then that was it for the rest

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:17.040
of the day.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:20.320
As far as official writing time I would pull
out a notebook or if I was really inspired

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:26.450
I might open up the computer and write some
more but those 2 hours I would set the timer

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:27.450
and I would write.

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:32.380
At the end of those two hours I would stop
no matter where I was, no matter if I felt

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:36.970
like I could keep going for another two or
if I was like “Thank God I just barely made

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:41.040
those two hours that felt like a slog.”

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:48.450
But having it be time-based told me that it
doesn’t matter what I’ve written as long

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:55.229
as I’ve shown up and I’ve written and
so some days those two hours would produce

00:01:55.229 --> 00:01:58.380
ten pages, other days it would produce one.

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:04.901
But I was measuring weather or not I was doing
my job by the time I was spending doing it

00:02:04.901 --> 00:02:07.760
and not necessarily page count or word count.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:15.590
I think so much of what we do is up here and
it doesn’t always translate to the pages

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:20.700
and so that one page that I wrote over those
two hours that might have been really difficult.

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:26.159
It was a lot of thinking and feeling and kind
of moving through what was going on there

00:02:26.159 --> 00:02:30.019
and that one page is great output for those
two hours.

00:02:30.019 --> 00:02:36.069
And those other ten pages I wrote the day
before might not be great.

00:02:36.069 --> 00:02:42.939
So I shouldn’t measure my progress with
what was going on the page but more about

00:02:42.939 --> 00:02:47.040
the fact that I could sit down, show up and
do the job.

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:51.949
Film Courage: Does active or passive voice
come into play in screenwriting?

00:02:51.949 --> 00:02:56.519
Or is this more of an issue with prose writing?

00:02:56.519 --> 00:03:04.730
David: I think active and passive voice doesn’t
come up in screenwriting for me.

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:12.249
I don’t know if that is because I’m just
not thinking about that as much.

00:03:12.249 --> 00:03:13.659
So I’m not sure?

00:03:13.659 --> 00:03:18.449
Film Courage: Okay…it’s just sometimes
they say don’t write in passive voice and

00:03:18.449 --> 00:03:23.930
I just wasn’t sure with the rules of screenwriting
is it a faux pas to write in passive voice

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:29.760
for a screenplay even if it’s just little
action lines?

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:40.669
David: I think one of the things that is great
about screenwriting is that there are no hard

00:03:40.669 --> 00:03:49.629
rules so if there is a reason that the passive
voice should used here for an effect it’s

00:03:49.629 --> 00:03:54.099
permissible, you can do whatever you want.

00:03:54.099 --> 00:03:59.690
I think understanding why this action line
is better in a passive voice as opposed to

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:04.639
an active voice or vice-versa is more important
than adhering to any sort of “You should

00:04:04.639 --> 00:04:07.219
never write in passive voice, you should never
do this.”

00:04:07.219 --> 00:04:13.949
Because for every rule that was laid out there
was a bunch of examples of it being broken

00:04:13.949 --> 00:04:20.909
and broken successfully and I think you can
break it successfully because you know why

00:04:20.909 --> 00:04:26.360
breaking it is going to produce some greater
effect than adhering to it and one of the

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:33.479
freedoms of the screenplay format is that
you have so much room to break all of these

00:04:33.479 --> 00:04:33.800
rules.

